<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=gear">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gear</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Cogwheel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A gear is a rotating machine part with teeth that engage the teeth of another part to transmit rotational motion or torque. The earliest surviving gears date to the 4th century BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and are preserved at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> Province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> mentions gears around 330 BCE, as wheel drives in windlasses, and observes that one gear wheel driving another reverses the direction of rotation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antikythera-mechanism">Antikythera mechanism</a>, estimated to date between 150 and 100 BCE, is the earliest surviving geared device in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Geared mechanical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clocks</a> were built in China by 725.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gear.jpg" alt="Gear" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frobles · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Engranajesreductores.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>4th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Luoyang, Henan, China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Greece (c. 330 BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Gears appear in works connected to Hero of Alexandria, but are traced back to the mechanics of the Library of Alexandria in the 3rd century BCE and to Archimedes (287 to 212 BCE); these are accounts of use, and the earliest surviving gears are the Chinese ones.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/wheel">Wheel<span>before 4000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/antikythera-mechanism">Antikythera mechanism<span>2nd century BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mechanical-clock">Mechanical clock<span>by 1300</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/pascal-s-calculator">Pascal's calculator<span>1642</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=gear">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
